eq":31,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:11:50.633340259Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":32,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:12:01.184807579Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -25"]},{"seq":33,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:12:37.039279381Z","text":"Several issues. Let me simplify drastically. The key fact: `eLpNorm' g 2 ≤ 1` implies `MemLp g 2` (since 1 < ∞). And the liminf of a sequence ≤ 1 is itself ≤ 1. Let me rewrite:"},{"seq":34,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:12:37.039695328Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":35,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:12:48.607241658Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":36,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:13:10.819818029Z","text":"Several issues. The `le_liminf_of_le` needs an autoParam and the `hf_bdd` argument is reversed. And `hfatou_ae_stronglyMeasurable` doesn't exist. Let me fix:\n\nFor the liminf: since all `eLpNorm (f n) 2 ≤ 1`, the liminf ≤ 1. The `le_liminf_of_le` takes `(hf : IsCoboundedUnder ... ) (h : ∀ᶠ n in f, a ≤ u n)` — I need `a = 1` and `1 ≤ eLpNorm (f n) 2`... but that's wrong. I need `eLpNorm (f n) 2 ≤ 1` (an upper bound), so the liminf is ≤ 1, which is `liminf_le_of_le` not `le_liminf_of_le`.\n\nActually, for the liminf of a sequence bounded above by 1, the liminf is ≤ 1. This follows from `liminf_le_of_le` which requires the coboundedness. Let me just use a simpler approach — since `eLpNorm' g 2 ≤ liminf eLpNorm' (f n) 2` and each `eLpNorm' (f n) 2 = eLpNorm (f n) 2 ≤ 1`, I need to show `liminf (≤ 1 values) ≤ 1`."},{"seq":37,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:13:10.820298681Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":38,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:13:31.977712731Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":39,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:13:52.910774253Z","text":"Three issues. The `liminf_le_of_le` and `aestronglyMeasurable_of_tendsto_ae` APIs are tricky. Let me commit what compiles and document the approach. The key difficulty is the liminf API coboundedness argument. Let me simplify to commit a partial:"},{"seq":40,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:13:52.911111486Z","tools":["write_file"],"previews":["write_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":41,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:13:53.068381943Z","text":"[auto-continue 1/8]"},{"seq":42,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:14:08.552682784Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":43,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:14:18.544173084Z","text":"The `aestronglyMeasurable_of_tendsto_ae` API is wrong. Let me check its signature:"},{"seq":44,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:14:18.548178974Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B1 -A8 \"theorem aestronglyMeasurable_of_tendsto_ae\\|lemma aestronglyMeasurable_of_te"]},{"seq":45,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:14:27.333779412Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -rn \"aestronglyMeasurable_of_tendsto\\|ae_stronglyMeasurable.*tendsto\\|AEStronglyMeasu"]},{"seq":46,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:14:34.763022625Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/.lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Function/StronglyMeasurable/AEStronglyMeasurable.le"]},{"seq":47,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:14:45.497870910Z","text":"The signature is: `aestronglyMeasurable_of_tendsto_ae (u : Filter ι) [NeBot u] [IsCountablyGenerated u] {f : ι → α → β} {g : α → β} (hf : ∀ i, AEStronglyMeasurable (f i) μ) (lim : ∀ᵐ x ∂μ, Tendsto ...) : AEStronglyMeasurable g μ`.\n\nSo it takes `(u : Filter ι)` first, then `hf`, then `lim`. My call was passing `hae` as the filter. Let me fix:"},{"seq":48,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:14:45.498289904Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":49,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:14:55.268926929Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":50,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:15:11.784514766Z","text":"Good — 2 sorries, compiles. The first sorry is the liminf ≤ 1 step. Let me try to close it. The key: I need to show `liminf (fun n => u n) atTop ≤ 1` when `u n ≤ 1` for all n. This should follow from `liminf_le_of_le` or similar. Let me try providing the coboundedness explicitly:"},{"seq":51,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:15:11.784975997Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":52,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:15:24.746614143Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":53,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:15:41.688408249Z","text":"The `eventually_atTop` rw fails because the filter is `map u atTop` not plain `atTop`. And the `liminf_le_of_le` coboundedness argument type doesn't match. The issue: `liminf_le_of_le` takes `IsCoboundedUnder` on the same filter, but the `h_cob` constructs it differently than needed. Let me construct it properly using the definition:"},{"seq":54,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:15:41.688772499Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":55,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:16:26.566125462Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":56,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:16:38.133366721Z","text":"Two issues: (1) `Filter.eventually_map` produces a non-inductive type, (2) `liminf_le_of_le` takes `IsBoundedUnder` not `IsCoboundedUnder`. The API confusion is significant. Let me check what `liminf_le_of_le` actually needs:"},{"seq":57,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:16:38.138871792Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B2 -A5 \"theorem liminf_le_of_le\\b\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/Order/LiminfLimsup"]},{"seq":58,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:16:56.443103464Z","text":"`liminf_le_of_le` takes `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u` and `(h : ∀ b, (∀ᶠ n in f, b ≤ u n) → b ≤ a)`. The first is an autoParam, and the second takes EACH `b` with `∀ᶠ n, b ≤ u n` and shows `b ≤ a`.\n\nFor our case: `a = 1`, `u n = eLpNorm (f n) 2`. I need: for any `b` with eventually `b ≤ eLpNorm (f n) 2`, show `b ≤ 1`. Since all `eLpNorm (f n) 2 ≤ 1`, any eventual lower bound `b` satisfies `b ≤ 1`."},{"seq":59,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:16:56.443968541Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":60,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:17:09.285500090Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -20"]},{"seq":61,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:17:18.836346821Z","text":"The autoParam `IsBoundedUnder` fails to resolve. I need to provide it. `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·) atTop u` means `∃ b, ∀ᶠ n in atTop, b ≥ u n`, i.e., bounded above. Since `u n ≤ 1`, this is `1` as the bound:"},{"seq":62,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:17:18.836940114Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":63,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:17:29.653063507Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":64,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:17:54.036924797Z","text":"The `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·)` requires `1 ≥ u n` which is `u n ≤ 1`. The issue is `(· ≥ ·)` vs `(· ≤ ·)`. `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u := IsBounded (· ≥ ·) (map u f)` which means `∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ x in map u f, a ≥ x) → a ≥ b`. Hmm, this is the \"cobounded\" direction. \n\nActually, for the liminf API, `liminf_le_of_le` takes `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·)` which is the condition that the liminf exists (bounded from below in the ≥ sense = bounded from above in the ≤ sense). For `u n ≤ 1`, the sequence is bounded above by 1, so the bound `b = 1` satisfies: any `a` with eventually `a ≥ u n` has `a ≥ u_n ≤ 1`, so... `a ≥ b`? Not necessarily.\n\nActually, `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u := ∃ b, ∀ᶠ x in f, b ≥ u x`. So I need `∃ b, ∀ᶠ n, b ≥ u n`, i.e., `∀ᶠ n, b ≥ eLpNorm (f n) 2`. With `b = 1` and `eLpNorm (f n) 2 ≤ 1`, this is `∀ᶠ n, 1 ≥ eLpNorm (f n) 2` which is `Eventually.of_forall hf_bdd` but with the inequality direction:"},{"seq":65,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:17:54.037457818Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":66,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:18:07.997476379Z","text":"Wait, that's still wrong. `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u` = `∃ b, ∀ᶠ x in f, b ≥ u x`. The `∀ᶠ x in f, b ≥ u x` means `∀ᶠ n in atTop, 1 ≥ eLpNorm (f n) 2` which is the same as `∀ᶠ n in atTop, eLpNorm (f n) 2 ≤ 1` = `Eventually.of_forall hf_bdd`. But the `≥` direction...\n\nLet me check the actual definition:"},{"seq":67,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:18:08.006171774Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && grep -B1 -A3 \"def IsBoundedUnder\\b\\|abbrev IsBoundedUnder\" .lake/packages/mathlib/Mathlib/"]},{"seq":68,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:18:18.912986500Z","text":"`IsBoundedUnder r f u = (map u f).IsBounded r`. And `IsBounded r f = ∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ x in f, r x a) → r b a`. So `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·) f u = ∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ x in map u f, x ≥ a) → b ≥ a`.\n\nThis is NOT simply \"bounded above\". It means: there exists `b` such that any `a` that is eventually a lower bound of `u` satisfies `a ≤ b`. In other words, the eventual lower bounds are bounded above by `b`. For `u n ≤ 1`, any `a ≤ 1` is eventually a lower bound, and `b = 1` works.\n\nSo `IsBoundedUnder (· ≥ ·) atTop u = ⟨1, fun a ha => ...⟩`. The `ha` is `∀ᶠ y in map u atTop, y ≥ a` which means eventually `u n ≥ a`. Then `1 ≥ a` follows from `a ≤ u n ≤ 1`.\n\nThis is the SAME cobounded issue from a93A03! Let me just construct it:"},{"seq":69,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:18:18.913719387Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":70,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:18:31.021604210Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":71,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:18:47.333264643Z","text":"The `IsBoundedUnder` is `IsBounded r (map u f)` which is `∃ b, ∀ a, ...`. But I'm providing `⟨1, fun a ha => ...⟩` where the second component should match `∀ a, (∀ᶠ ...) → 1 ≥ a`. The issue: `IsBoundedUnder` expects `IsBounded r (map u f)` which has `∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ x in (map u f), r x a) → r b a`. My `⟨1, fun a ha => ...⟩` should provide `b = 1` and the proof. But the `∃` uses `IsBounded` not a plain pair. Let me check:\n\nActually `IsBounded` is defined as `∃ b, ∀ a, ...`. So `⟨1, fun a ha => proof⟩` IS the right form for an `∃`. The error suggests the types don't match because `IsBoundedUnder` expands to `(map u f).IsBounded r` which is `∃ b, ∀ a, (∀ᶠ x in map u f, r x a) → r b a`.\n\nThe error message says my `fun a ha => ...` is being interpreted as `∀ᶠ x in map ..., r x 1` not `∀ a, (∀ᶠ ...) → r 1 a`. The issue is likely a unification problem with the autoParam. Let me provide it as a term:"},{"seq":72,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:18:47.334066180Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":73,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:18:57.876856895Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":74,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:19:08.595401724Z","text":"The `have` and `apply` are separate. Let me fix the indentation:"},{"seq":75,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:19:08.595878627Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":76,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:19:20.539934774Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm-lean && lake env lean problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean 2>&1 | head -15"]},{"seq":77,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:19:30.572796361Z","text":"There's a parsing issue — the `have` statements need to be at the right indentation level. Let me check the current file structure:"},{"seq":78,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:19:30.573587931Z","tools":["read_file"],"previews":["read_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":79,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:19:30.754859703Z","text":"[auto-continue 2/8]"},{"seq":80,"type":"text","at":"2026-07-27T06:19:44.521002611Z","text":"The indentation is wrong — there's a duplicate `have h_liminf_le` line. Let me fix the file:"},{"seq":81,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:19:44.521592761Z","tools":["edit_file"],"previews":["edit_file /home/joe/code/apm-lean/problems/a96A03/lean/Main.lean"]},{"seq":82,"type":"tool_use","at":"2026-07-27T06:19:58.160821586Z","tools":["run_shell"],"previews":["run_shell cd /home/joe/code/apm